NOTES["kinetic_ctl"] = dict(desc="KINETIC motion controller main board (112 x 80 mm): ESP32-S3 module with native USB, three StepStick-style stepper-driver sockets (A4988 / DRV8825 / TMC2209 modules), 24 V input with TVS and reverse-polarity PMOS, 5 V buck (AP63205) and 3.3 V LDO, three endstop inputs, SPI header for the vibration sensor and a host UART.",
  circuit=["**Power:** 12-28 V in on a 5.08 mm terminal; SMBJ28A TVS; AO3401A reverse-polarity PMOS with a 12 V zener gate clamp (so it survives 24 V); 100 uF + 10 uF bulk; AP63205 buck makes 5 V (4.7 uH, 2 x 22 uF); AP2112K LDO makes 3.3 V for the ESP32 (600 mA limit: do not enable heavy WiFi traffic while all axes run, or add a bigger LDO). USB VBUS joins +5V through an SS14 so the board can be programmed without the motor supply.", "**Drivers:** each of the three sockets takes a 16-pin StepStick module. ENABLE, STEP, DIR come from the ESP32; MS1-3 are tied high (1/16 step on A4988, 1/32 on DRV8825) and RESET/SLEEP are pulled up. VMOT is the switched 24 V rail. Motor leads go to JST-PH 4 connectors (rated about 2 A per pin; set the module current limit accordingly).", "**Inputs:** three endstops (1 k + 100 nF filtered, pull-ups in firmware), battery/supply sense divider (100 k / 10 k) on IO1, native USB on IO19/IO20, boot and reset buttons, status LED, host UART on a JST-PH 3.", "**Sensor:** 6-pin SPI header (3.3 V, GND, SCLK, MOSI, MISO, CS) for the KINETIC vibration puck."],
  sims=[], power="Idle about 0.1 A at 24 V (ESP32, buck quiescent, LED). Each driver adds its motor current; the buck is limited to 2 A on the 5 V side (logic only).",
  bringup=["Before fitting any driver: apply 24 V with a current-limited supply; TP1 = 24 V, TP2 = 5.0 V, TP3 = 3.3 V.", "Plug USB: the ESP32-S3 enumerates; flash the firmware.", "Fit ONE driver module with the motor DISCONNECTED, set its current limit (Vref), then connect the motor. Never plug or unplug a motor with power on.", "Run the `kn_motion` self-test move and the endstop check before connecting all axes."])
